+The identity `eta=0 => T2=0` should not be used in the free ring. There it would assert that `-(9/4)l2^2` vanishes whenever eta=0, which is false. At the verified varieties l2 also vanishes, and that additional fact supplies the implication. Likewise the prompt’s phrase `eta != 0 (u != 0)` is not a ring identity: eta=1,l2=-4/3 has u=0.
+
+**6. Uniform theorem: scheme thickness forces unbounded exponents.**
+
+Define a ring map, for each N >= 4,
+
+```
+rho_N:S_N -> Q[z],
+u -> 4z, B -> 0, w2 -> 0, every l_j -> 0.
+```
+
+It sends eta to z and L to -1. Specialization commutes with the recursion, which uses only scalar denominators. The differential equation becomes
+
+```
+(theta-3)(P^2)=-3/16+(z/2)x^2.
+```
+
+Set H=P². Solving coefficientwise gives `H=1/16-(z/2)x²+c*x³`. The marked P0=-1/4 and P3=w2=0 force c=0. Hence the unique marked series is exactly
+
+```
+P=-1/4*sqrt(1-8z*x^2),
+rho_N(Phi_(2r))=c_r*z^r,
+c_r=-(1/4)*binom(1/2,r)*(-8)^r != 0,
+rho_N(Phi_(2r+1))=0.
+```
+
+Every c_r is a nonzero rational number. The even closing indices are `2N+2,2N+4,...,4N`, so their exponents are N+1 through 2N. Their generated ideal is therefore exactly
+
+```
+rho_N(I_close,N)=(z^(N+1)).                         (THICK)
+```
+
+If `eta^e in I_close,N`, applying rho_N gives `z^e in (z^(N+1))`, so e >= N+1; the same holds for u. Two observations 19 -> 27 do not exclude every fixed bound, whereas (THICK) already does. The following construction strengthens this and also treats B*eta.
+
+**Theorem 6.1: e >= 2N, including the residual target B*eta.** Work over `A_N=Q[z]/(z^(2N))`, and set B=1, L=-1, eta=z. Put A=-1/4-x and seek
+
+```
+P=A+sum_(r=1)^(2N-1) z^r*f_r(x),  f_1=x^2.
+T(f)=-(1/2)*(theta-3)f-x*(2theta-3)f.
+```
+
+The constant term in z satisfies (UF), and `T(x^2)=x^2/2-x^3` gives the required linear term. For r >= 2 define
+
+```
+T(f_r)=-(theta-3)*sum_(i=1)^(r-1) f_i*f_(r-i).
+```
+
+Inductively the right side is divisible by x^4 and has degree at most r+2. The operator T is an isomorphism from the span of `x^3,...,x^(r+1)` to the span of `x^4,...,x^(r+2)`: on a monomial
+
+```
+T(x^j)=-(j-3)*x^j/2-(2j-3)*x^(j+1).
+```
+
+This matrix is triangular with nonzero diagonal `-(2j-3)`, j=3,...,r+1. Thus f_r exists uniquely, is divisible by x^3, and has degree at most r+1. For example `f_2=x^3/3`, `f_3=4x^4/15-2x^3/45`.
+
+The resulting P has degree at most 2N and satisfies (UF) modulo z^(2N), with exactly the marked constant, linear and quadratic jets. Assign `w2=[x^3]P`, u=4z, and every l_j=0. Lemma 2.1 works over this nonreduced Q-algebra because all recursive pivots are units. It therefore defines a ring map `S_N/I_close,N -> A_N` sending eta and B*eta to z, and u to 4z. Their powers of order 2N-1 are nonzero. This proves the claimed lower bound, even after localizing at B. For T2 powers it gives e >= N. The construction and its residual were independently checked exactly at N=4,5,6 in `theory_artin_thickness.py`.
+
+This is not a proof that every conceivable parameterized certificate method fails. A formula with an exponent depending on N remains possible. It is a lower bound on the exponent and hence on the degree of these ordinary power-membership targets. It says nothing by itself about the optimal cofactor representation.
+
+Nor does (THICK) give a reduced point with z != 0. Its reduction is z=0. For z != 0 the displayed square root is not a polynomial: its square has degree two and no linear term, and cannot be the square of a polynomial with the marked nonzero constant term and zero linear coefficient. The termination argument succeeds completely on this restricted slice. What grows with N is its infinitesimal thickness. Thus increasing nilpotence exponents are compatible with an unchanged reduced curve and do not prove that new components appear.
+
+**7. Why the proposed general triangular-series argument does not finish.**
+
+For a fixed P and a chosen l2, the charged L-side recursion on u != 0 has, at b=1, the form
+
+```
+l_(k-2) = 4*rest_k/u - 2*(k-3)*P_k/u,   k >= 5.
+```
+
+The coefficient of the newest P_k is nonzero. Already l3 varies with P5, l4 with P6, and so on. At degree bound N, the coefficients P5,...,P_(N+2) form a growing list of inputs before both polynomiality constraints have been imposed. The recursion is triangular conditional on those inputs; it does not identify the tail of one fixed series independent of N. Proving that **every** series arising from those choices fails to truncate simultaneously with L is precisely a further polynomial-termination theorem.
+
+In addition, this chart misses the whole possibility `eta != 0, u=0`. On that locus `l2=-4eta/3`, and the next pivot is governed by `2B*l2+l3+2w2`, as in the charged pivot chain. A proof on u != 0 cannot be applied to that locus by continuity or by calling eta the pivot. None of these free-jet examples is being presented as a closing solution; they identify the invalid chart implication before closing is imposed.
+
+The charged conjugate branch supplies no substitute termination argument. Tacnode Theorem 3.1 establishes that for a polynomial solution of actual degree at least three the second branch is algebraic and nonpolynomial, not another polynomial solution. That fact is consistent with the existence of the original polynomial branch. A Wronskian or polynomiality assertion about the conjugate would require a new hypothesis or theorem. No such assertion is introduced here.
+
+The successful square-root calculation in §6 is therefore a controlled special case, not a generic model silently applied to every L. It proves an exact obstruction to fixed exponents, while leaving the growing family of L and P coefficients visible.
